  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 322 AM EDT SUN OCT 25 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Monday evening with a flattening 500 \nhPa ridge extending from the Gulf of America northward across the \nCommonwealth and beyond to the Upper Great Lakes, then northwest \ninto Central Canada. At the surface, the remnant of a former high \nwill be situated over the Central Appalachians, separated from a \nstronger ~1034 mb surface high over Northern Ontario by a cold \nfront. That frontal boundary extends southwestward from a low over\nthe Labrador Sea, passing along the southern shores of the Great \nLakes to a weak surface low over Nebraska. Aloft, the Nebraskan low\nis supported by a shortwave trough ejecting northeast out of a \nbroader parent trough over the West Coast and Intermountain West. \nA second embedded shortwave trough is located over the Pacific \nNorthwest.\n\nThe first shortwave trough will merge with the second and eject \nnortheast into Central Canada through Wednesday, taking the \nsurface low with them and pulling the stalled front back north of \nthe Ohio Valley. The main effect from those features will be to \nsignificantly flatten the ridge residing over the Commonwealth. An\narea of energy likely ejects northeast and eventually crosses \neastern Kentucky as an upper-level disturbance late Tuesday into \nWednesday, bringing mid-and high-level moisture (likely \nmanifesting as just an uptick in cloud cover at most). Behind that\ndisturbance, the leftover broad ridging over the eastern CONUS \nwill remain nearly steady state or strengthen slight through \nFriday. Surface high pressure scoots southeast to off the New\nEngland Coast by Wednesday evening and then builds southwestward\ninto the Central/Southern Appalachians through ~12Z Friday.\n\nThereafter, a much more substantial shortwave ejects northeast\nnext weekend with a plume of Gulf moisture (PWATs surge back \nabove an inch and likely exceed the 90th percentile of climo for \nthis time of year). The return of deep moisture with this feature\nwill manifest as thickening cloud cover heralding rising rain \nchances late in the day on Saturday--potentially more of a trick \nrather than a treat given the delightful weather of late. \n\nOutside of the periods of thicker cloud cover mentioned, dry air \nwill allow for efficient radiational cooling and substantial ~10F\nridge-valley splits each night. Temperatures may be just cold \nenough for one more night of patchy frost in the coldest hollows \non Monday night. Otherwise, slightly milder temperatures should \nhopefully preclude further patchy frost. However, conditions will \nremain favorable for nighttime fog formation in the typically \nfavored valley locales.\n\nThus in sensible weather terms, expect mostly clear skies for \nmuch of the upcoming week outside of a period of greater cloud \ncover Tuesday into Wednesday and again heading through Saturday. \nAdditionally, temperatures will slowly moderate, warming from the \nmid 70s for highs on Tuesday to the mid to upper 70s by week's \nend. Meanwhile, nighttime lows will range from the mid 30s to near\n50F on Monday night to the mid 40s to mid 50s by Friday night. \nGreater cloud cover on Tuesday night should favor milder temperatures\nthan what would otherwise be expected in late October. Any rain \nchances will hold off until Saturday at the earliest.",
